[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Building Scalable Design Systems with CVA and Tailwind Merge"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building component libraries with Tailwind CSS, managing variant states and resolving class conflicts can quickly become complex. This tutorial explores how to combine Class Variance Authority (CVA) and Tailwind Merge to build robust, scalable, and type-safe UI components."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Problem: Class Conflicts and Variant Sprawl"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"As applications grow, UI components need to support multiple variants like sizes, colors, and states. Manually writing conditional strings for these variants leads to messy code. Furthermore, allowing users to pass custom className props often results in conflicting Tailwind classes where the later ones do not override the defaults due to CSS specificity issues."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Installation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To get started, install the required packages in your project using your preferred package manager."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"npm install class-variance-authority tailwind-merge clsx"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Utility Helper"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, create a utility function that combines clsx and tailwind-merge. This ensures that conditional classes work correctly and conflicting Tailwind classes (like bg-red-500 and bg-blue-500) are intelligently resolved."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import { type ClassValue, clsx } from "clsx";
import { twMerge } from "tailwind-merge";

export function cn(...inputs: ClassValue[]) {
  return twMerge(clsx(inputs));
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Component Variants with CVA"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"CVA allows you to create a predictable API for component variants. Here is how you can build a flexible Button component."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import React from "react";
import { cva, type VariantProps } from "class-variance-authority";
import { cn } from "./utils";

const buttonVariants = cva(
  "inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md text-sm font-medium transition-colors focus-visible:outline-none disabled:opacity-50",
  {
    variants: {
      variant: {
        default: "bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/90",
        destructive: "bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground hover:bg-destructive/90",
        outline: "border border-input bg-background hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground",
      },
      size: {
        default: "h-10 px-4 py-2",
        sm: "h-9 rounded-md px-3",
        lg: "h-11 rounded-md px-8",
      },
    },
    defaultVariants: {
      variant: "default",
      size: "default",
    },
  }
);"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing the Component Interface"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, define the component props by extending standard HTML button attributes and the variants generated by CVA."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"export interface ButtonProps
  extends React.ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>,
    VariantProps<typeof buttonVariants> {
  asChild?: boolean;
}

export const Button = React.forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, ButtonProps>(
  ({ className, variant, size, ...props }, ref) => {
    return (
      <button
        className={cn(buttonVariants({ variant, size, className }))}
        ref={ref}
        {...props}
      />
    );
  }
);
Button.displayName = "Button";"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always pass the custom className prop through the cn utility at the very end of your CVA function call."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use compound variants in CVA when certain props depend on combinations of other variant values."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your base styles minimal and let variants handle specific design tokens."}]
